An element that already carries a Primer `ActionMenu` can offer that same menu as a context menu, so a right-click on the element opens its actions instead of the browser's own menu. Opt in with the `contextual-action-menu` Stimulus controller — nothing else changes, and the menu keeps its show button.

<%= embed Patterns::ContextualActionMenuPreview, :default, panels: %i[source] %>

Put the controller on a wrapper you own rather than on the `action-menu` element itself, and give the wrapper a tab stop so the keyboard entry points can reach it:

```erb
<%%= render(Primer::Box.new(
      tabindex: 0,
      aria: { label: "…", keyshortcuts: "Shift+F10" },
      data: { controller: "contextual-action-menu" }
    )) do %>
  <%%# … card content, including a Primer::Alpha::ActionMenu … %>
<%% end %>
```

Attach it alongside whatever controller already owns the element's ordinary behaviour — the two do not interact.

## What it does

* **Right-click** on the element opens the menu at the pointer, anchored on the element so it keeps following it while the page scrolls.
* **Shift+F10** and the **Context Menu key** open the menu where its own show button opens it, and hand focus back to the element when it closes. Announce the shortcut with `aria-keyshortcuts="Shift+F10"`; the dedicated Context Menu key needs no announcement.
* A right-click on the **show button** opens the menu where that button always opens it.

## What it deliberately does not do

* **Interactive descendants keep the browser's menu.** Links, buttons, form fields and the open menu itself all fall through, so copying a link address still works.
* **Touch and pen keep the native long-press menu.** There is no touch-sized design for the menu, so the gesture is left to the browser.
* **It never touches the menu's contents.** The controller adds entry points only; what the menu offers, and any selection state, stays with whatever else owns the element.

## Preparing or suppressing an invocation

The controller dispatches a cancelable `contextual-action-menu:beforeOpen` event carrying `{ origin: 'pointer' | 'keyboard' }`. Calling `preventDefault()` on it suppresses the invocation and hands the gesture back to the browser.

Note that the event means *an invocation was attempted*, not *a menu is about to appear*: it fires at gesture time, while the pointer path opens the menu later, at the release. A listener may therefore see it without any menu following, or twice for a single menu. Keep such listeners idempotent.
